+ * @brief Applies the primary column's width constraints, resizing a displayed split over several frames.
+ *
+ * The width follows layout that changes without a UIKit transition, such as a foldable's hinge settling. An animation
+ * block would only move the native containers: this runs while a React mount transaction is in flight, so the frames
+ * that split screens commit mount after the block, at their final size. Laying out each intermediate width from a
+ * display link lets every frame mount synchronously instead, so content reflows with its column as in a window resize.
+ * The new width arrives after the system has started its own posture transition, so the resize eases out to settle as
+ * that transition clears rather than easing in behind it.
+ *
+ * A width that follows the split's own size, as in a window resize, is applied at once: the resize already moves the
+ * columns, and restarting a resize on each of its steps would leave the column trailing behind.
+ */
+- (void)updatePrimaryColumnFor:(id<RNSSplitHostAppearanceProvider>)provider
+                withController:(RNSSplitHostController *)splitHostController
